# Memory

## Single cell

```forth
variable v   ( -- addr )
5 v !  v @ .
```

`variable` allots one cell; you cannot `allot` more to it in standard Forth.

## Buffers with create

```forth
create buf  20 cells allot    ( -- addr )
3 buf 5 cells + !             \ store at index 5
```

Initialize inline with `,`:

```forth
create vals  5 , 4 , 3 , 2 , 1 ,
vals @ .  vals cell+ @ .
```

### Indexed buffers (challenge / fixed-size data)

Reuse one base and index with `cells` / `chars` — stack contract **`( value index -- )` for store**, **`( index -- value )` for fetch**:

```forth
create ch-data  ch-max cells allot

: ch@  ( i -- n )       cells ch-data + @ ;
: ch!  ( n i -- )       swap cells ch-data + ! ;
```

Do not use `tuck` on `ch!` unless **three** stack items are present (`value`, `index`, and any extra the word documents). Wrong order → segfault or garbage.

### Parallel arrays (fake linked lists / graphs)

When the scaffold gives `ch-vals` + `ch-nexts` (or `x`/`y` pairs), **index = node id**; `-1` (or `0` per spec) = no link. No heap nodes — walk with `next @` on the index field only:

```forth
\ val@ / next@ — your ch@ on parallel tables ch-vals and ch-nexts
: walk ( head -- )
  begin  dup -1 <> while
    dup val@ .   next@
  repeat  drop ;
```

Same idea for BFS/DP grids: separate `create` tables per dimension, not one overloaded name per cell.

## Singly-linked lists (heap or dictionary nodes)

When parallel arrays are not enough, use **nodes linked by address**. Keep the head in a **`variable`** (stable address for insert/shift):

```forth
\ Node: first cell = next (0 = end); payload in following cells or inline

: list-prepend ( node head-var -- )
  2dup @ swap !  ! ;          \ node.next := old head; head := node

: list-shift ( head-var -- node )
  dup @ dup >r @ swap !  r> ; \ pop head; advance head to next

: list-walk ( xt head-var -- )
  >r r@ @ begin dup while
    dup r@ swap r@ swap execute if r> 2drop exit then
    @
  repeat r> drop drop ;
```

`list-walk` calls `( node head-var -- flag )`; **true** stops. Guard nil before `@` when the caller might dereference an empty list.

### Dictionary-linked push (Rosetta Code — verbatim)

`Singly-linked-list-Element-definition/singly-linked-list-element-definition-1.fth` — **ok** in gforth; nodes live in the dictionary (not `free`able):

```forth
0 value numbers
: push ( n -- )
  here swap numbers , , to numbers ;
```

For challenge linked lists prefer parallel arrays or theForthNet `ll` — see § above.

### Sorted lookup (Rosetta Code — gforth fix)

`gforth/binary-search.fth` — upstream uses `-cell` (broken). Linear `find-idx`; with locals use `addr# i cells + @`, not `i addr# cells + @`:

```forth
: find-idx ( key addr u -- idx|-1 )
  { key# addr# u# }  -1 { idx# }
  u# 0 do
    addr# i cells + @ key# = if i to idx# then
  loop
  idx# ;
```

### Fixed cell array (Rosetta Code — gforth substitute)

`gforth/collections-minimal.fth` — upstream needs `ffl/car.fs`. Dictionary-backed array:

```forth
create arr  10 cells allot
: arr@ ( i -- n )  cells arr + @ ;
: arr! ( n i -- )  cells arr + ! ;
```

`bounds` for `( c-addr u -- end start )` — use with `?do`/`+loop` over a cell region:

```forth
: bounds ( c-addr u -- end start )  over + swap ;
```

## Dictionary memory (here / allot)

```forth
here 10 cells allot   ( -- start )
here                  ( -- end )
```

`allot` uses dictionary memory like a stack — roll back only if no new word was defined in between:

```forth
-10 cells allot
```

Store the start address; `here` alone does not give you a named handle.

## Heap (any order free)

```forth
10 cells allocate throw   ( -- addr )
free throw
```

Check every `ior`; use `throw` in application code. See `forth-io.mdc` for the same pattern on file words.

## Address arithmetic

Use `cell+` / `cells` for cell arrays; `char+` / `chars` for byte buffers. Do not hard-code `2*` for cell scaling — see `forth-portability.mdc`.

## Alignment

`create` and `allocate` start at aligned addresses. `char+` may produce unaligned addresses — use `aligned` before `@` / `!`:

```forth
create raw  97 c,
raw char+ aligned @    \ safe cell fetch
```

Use `align` before `,` when mixing `c,` and cell stores in one `create` block.

## Arrays vs parameter stack

Prefer `create` / `allot` / `variable` buffers over treating the data stack as a random-access array. See `forth-anti-patterns.mdc`.

## Memory block copy

For `( c-addr u )` buffers — source before destination in `move`/`cmove`/`cmove>`:

```forth
: copy-chars ( src dst u -- )
  cmove ;

: copy-overlap-low ( src dst u -- )
  cmove ;          \ safe when dst <= src

: copy-overlap-high ( src dst u -- )
  cmove> ;         \ safe when dst >= src
```

`move` works on address units (often prefix `chars` for char buffers). `blank` / `erase` fill with spaces / zero bits.
